The Markov comparator falls short in a different way. It reproduces the number of transitions almost exactly, at an error of 0.264 transitions per day against a tolerance of 1.50. It misses dwell times by an order of magnitude, at 119.6 minutes against 10.0. Matching a rate while missing the durations is the typical first-order shortfall, and it is why dwell times are scored at all.

The grammar is essential. Without it, only 6.88, 30.96 and 10.65 per cent of diaries are structurally valid in Spain, the UK and Italy, against a requirement of 99.90 per cent. The mask had to intervene in 69 to 93 per cent of the generated population. The grammar is also not neutral. At 5,169, 5,066 and 5,065 valid diaries against a target of 5,200, the decoding-neutrality check misses its tolerance of 5.0 minutes per day in all three folds. The worst activity code is off by 29.60 to 101.02 minutes. No claim in this paper depends on neutral decoding.

## 3.5 Appliance timing

Table 5 gives the hour of the stock appliance-electricity peak for three diary sources run through the same appliance model. The households, calendar, seeds, trigger probabilities and appliance parameters are identical; only the diaries differ. Unweighted real diaries put the evening peak at 18:00 to 21:00: 18:00 in the UK, 19:00 in Italy and 21:00 in Spain. The generated diaries give a different order: 14:00 in Spain, 18:00 in Italy and 20:00 in the UK. The raked donor diaries put all three peaks at 19:00 to 21:00. Neither synthetic source therefore carries country timing.

